  "response": "以下は、指定された要件を満たす Python 関数 `format_currency(n)` です。\n\n```python\ndef format_currency(n):\n    if n < 0:\n        return \"-\" + \"$\" + \"{:,}\".format(abs(n))\n    else:\n        return \"$\" + \"{:,}\".format(n)\n```\n\n### 説明：\n- `\"${abs(n):,}\"` で整数をフォーマットし、3 桁ごとにカンマで区切られます。\n- 負の数の場合は、マイナス記号 `-` をドル記号 `$` の前に配置します。\n- `f\"{n:,}\"` や `\"${n:,}\"` などでも同様の結果が得られますが、この実装はシンプルで読みやすいものです。\n\n### テスト例：\n\n```python\nprint(format_currency(4640039))    # \"$4,640,039\"\nprint(format_currency(-12345678))  # \"-$12,345,678\"\nprint(format_currency(0))          # \"$0\"\nprint(format_currency(9999999))    # \"$9,999,999\"\n```\n\nこの関数は、任意の整数（正・負・ゼロ）に対しても正しい形式化された文字列を返すようになっています。",
  "thinking": "The user wants me to create a Python function called `format_currency(n)` that:\n1. Takes an integer/number as input\n2. Adds a dollar sign ($) at the beginning\n3. Separates every 3 digits with commas (thousands separator)\n4. For negative numbers, puts a minus sign before the dollar sign\n\nLet me think about how to implement this:\n\n1. Handle negative numbers - check if n < 0 and prepend \"-\"\n2. Convert number to string\n3. Use Python's built-in formatting or regex to add commas every 3 digits\n4. Add the $ at the beginning\n\nPython has built-in ways to format numbers with thousands separators:\n- `f\"{n:,}\"` or `\"${n:,}\"` \n- `format(n, \",\")`\n\nHowever, I need to be careful about negative numbers and the order of symbols.\n\nLet me write a solution:\n\n```python\ndef format_currency(n):\n    sign = \"-\" if n < 0 else \"\"\n    abs_n = abs(n)\n    formatted = f\"$\" + \"{:,}\".format(abs_n)\n    \n    # For negative numbers, need to handle properly\n    # Actually let me reconsider\n```\n\nWait, I think the cleaner approach is:\n1. Check if negative, store sign\n2. Work with absolute value for formatting\n3. Combine sign and $ at the beginning\n\nLet me test the logic:\n- 4640039 -> \"$4,640,039\"\n- -4640039 -> \"-$4,640,039\" (based on requirement \"ドル記号の前にマイナス記号\")\n\n```python\ndef format_currency(n):\n    if n < 0:\n        return \"-\" + \"$\" + \"{:,}\".format(abs(n))\n    else:\n        return \"$\" + \"{:,}\".format(n)\n```\n\nOr more simply:\n```python\ndef format_currency(n):\n    formatted = f\"${abs(n):,}\"\n    if n < 0:\n        formatted = \"-\" + formatted\n    return formatted\n\n\n```\n\nBut this would give \"$-4640039\" which isn't right. Let me adjust the approach to place the minus sign before the dollar sign. The cleanest solution handles the sign separately and appends it to the beginning of the formatted number with the dollar sign. I'll check if the number is negative first, then format accordingly.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The example doesn't show a negative case, but the requirement says minus goes before dollar. So for -4640039 it should be \"-$4,640,039\".\n\n```python\ndef format_currency(n):\n    sign = \"-\" if n < 0 else \"\"\n    return f\"{sign}$\" + \"{:,}\".format(abs(n))\n```\n\nThis should work. Let me verify the logic handles both positive and negative cases correctly by checking the formatting of absolute value and prepending the appropriate sign and dollar symbol.\n\n```python\ndef format_currency(n):\n    if n < 0:\n        return \"-\" + \"$\" + \"{:,}\".format(abs(n))\n    else:\n        return \"$\" + \"{:,}\".format(n)\n```\n\nI could also use string formatting with a simpler approach.",
